  "finish": {
    "requirements": [
      {
        "requirement": "A single hex base",
        "status": "met",
        "evidence": "Just one base: piece:0,0,0;half-s (a half base, the only kind that takes a drawer)."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Nothing else added beside or on top of it",
        "status": "met",
        "evidence": "No other bases, covers, caps, bolts or props. The one other part is the drawer inside the base. I skipped capOpenFaces and lockJoints on purpose to follow this request."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Five drawers in it",
        "status": "unmet",
        "evidence": "Only one drawer fits (drawer:0#0,0#cut). With one drawer in, find_sockets offers no more drawer spots. Full and quarter bases offered none at all."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "The configurator won't let one base hold five drawers. A drawer only fits on the cut face of a half base, and a half base has just one cut face, so it takes one drawer. I tested the other options. A full base offered no drawer spot. A quarter base offered none either. A 3-storey quarter stack offered none, and it would have meant stacking bases anyway, which the brief rules out. So I built the closest thing that works: one South-half hex base (piece:0,0,0;half-s) with one drawer (drawer:0#0,0#cut) in its cut face. I left off the end caps and joint bolts that the house rules normally add, because you said nothing else should be added. There's one open face that could take a cap. check_build passes with no rule errors, and the mesh-collision check came back clear. Getting five drawers would need more bases beside or on top, for example five half bases each with its own drawer, or notch drawers between bases that touch.",
    "outcome": "compromise"
  },
